[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should logs end up in production? → Console + remote sink (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good. Next: what the browser side actually captures.

  The fork is whether the logger only records what we explicitly tell it to, or also installs global handlers. Most
  production issues you'd want to debug are the ones nobody wrote a log line for — a thrown exception in an event
  handler, a rejected fetch. Explicit-only logging misses exactly those.

  - [1mExplicit calls only[0m — [38;5;153mlogger.info/warn/error[39m at hand-picked call sites. Simplest, no global state, zero surprise.
    Misses uncaught errors entirely, so the crash you most want to see never reaches the sink.
  - [1mExplicit + global error capture[0m — additionally hooks [38;5;153mwindow.onerror[39m and [38;5;153munhandledrejection[39m in the browser, and
    [38;5;153muncaughtException[39m/[38;5;153munhandledRejection[39m in Node. Catches the unwritten cases with stack traces. Costs a small amount of
    global setup and a decision about whether Node should exit after logging a fatal. This is the part that's awkward
    to retrofit cleanly, since it affects module initialization order.
  - [1mExplicit + globals + breadcrumbs[0m — also keeps a rolling in-memory buffer of recent events, attached to any error
    that ships, so you see what led up to the crash. Most debuggable, most machinery.

  My recommendation: [1mexplicit + global error capture[0m. It's the smallest design that actually satisfies "debug production
  issues," and breadcrumbs can be layered onto the same buffer later if the error reports turn out to lack context.
